## Checkpoint 04A completed findings

Canonical interpretation: `docs/CHECKPOINT_04A_FINDINGS.md`. Raw/generated evidence:
`reports/checkpoint_04a/`.

Key results:

```text
adversarial train-vs-target AUC                    0.4892
Spearman(geo distance, |delta yield|)             0.4882
Spearman(C1 agronomic distance, |delta yield|)    0.2200
Spearman(C4 deterministic distance, |delta yield|)0.1833
Moran's I observed yield                           0.6797  p=0.001
Moran's I E123 grouped residual                    0.5483  p=0.001
Moran's I E123 state-stratified residual          -0.0375 p=0.429
support taxonomy                                   A=5 B=2 C=5 D=13 E=34
```

Interpretation: there is little evidence of a global train/target distribution shift, but very
strong spatial yield structure and heterogeneous parcel-level support. Geography is the
strongest observed similarity-transfer signal. Monthly temporal similarity alone is weak.
04B should therefore prioritize target-matched spatial/local validation, support-tier-aware
global/local mixtures, graph methods and explicit testing of SIAP/local external priors.

A display-only bug in the original generated 04A report labeled the expected direction of
`temporal_pearson_mean` as positive. The statistic is correct; the useful direction is
negative because higher Pearson similarity should imply smaller absolute yield difference.
The code has been fixed for future runs.


## Checkpoint 04B implementation status

04B code/config/tests are committed. The run will compare:

```text
GlobalMean / StateMean / municipality shrinkage
GeoKNN k=3/5/10
Agronomic-kNN
mixed geographic+agronomic kNN
one mixed geographic+agronomic+temporal kNN
Global Ridge on transductive C1 PCs
Global PLS on transductively preprocessed C0
query-specific Local Ridge
graph-Laplacian regression
fixed global/local blends
```

Primary pseudo masks contain 41 parcels, matching the 59/197 hidden fraction after scaling to
the 138 labeled universe. Target-matched mask construction uses only state/municipality and
X-derived geographic/agronomic/temporal/adversarial support descriptors. It never receives y.

04B also creates a new dynamic X-only support score. This is the support quantity allowed for
method routing. The 04A support score included labeled-neighbor-y consistency and therefore
must not be used to select pseudo-target masks or route methods during pseudo-validation.

Expected outputs include every pseudo prediction, per-split metrics, protocol summaries,
support-tier RMSE, robustness tables, actual-target X-only support, and a 59-row method-routing
proposal with no target yield prediction.


## Checkpoint 04B completed findings

Canonical interpretation: `docs/CHECKPOINT_04B_FINDINGS.md`. Generated evidence:
`reports/checkpoint_04b/`.

Key results:

```text
target-matched LocalRidge k20 alpha100       RMSE 0.4946
target-matched Graph k8 lambda2              RMSE 0.4974
target-matched LocalRidge k30 alpha100       RMSE 0.4976
target-matched GeoKNN k10                    RMSE 0.5021
target-matched GlobalPLS C0 4                RMSE 0.5379

Graph k8 lambda2:
  state-random                               RMSE 0.4655
  frozen state-stratified                    RMSE 0.4779
  frozen municipality-grouped               RMSE 0.5466
  worst-family                               RMSE 0.5466
```

LocalRidge k20 and Graph k8 are effectively near-tied on the 16 primary splits; LocalRidge wins
11/16 but only by mean RMSE 0.0028. Graph k8 is substantially more robust under the
municipality-grouped stress protocol.

Target-matched masks are meaningfully closer to the real 59-target X geometry than state-random
masks (municipality TV 0.0547 vs 0.0889; profile distance 0.5369 vs 0.9227).

The 04B support-tier routing proposal is exploratory only. It selected tier winners on the same
pseudo-test evidence used to compare methods and therefore needs leave-one-split-out or nested
validation before final routing. The X-only support score is also not a monotone uncertainty
measure: mid-support pseudo-targets were harder than low-support pseudo-targets.

Next priority:
1. tune graph topology/k/lambda and local Ridge neighborhood/regularization;
2. test residual graph correction on top of a global anchor;
3. validate any routing/gating rule out of sample;
4. run a focused high-compute CatBoost global anchor;
5. test SIAP 2025 municipality-level evidence as a local prior/node feature.


## Checkpoint 04D.1 implementation status

The local/graph refinement runner is committed:

```text
configs/checkpoint04d1.yaml
src/geocebada/evaluation/checkpoint04d1.py
src/geocebada/visualization/checkpoint04d1.py
tools/run_checkpoint_04d1.py
tests/test_checkpoint04d1.py
reports/checkpoint_04d1/README.md
```

The exhaustive primary grid contains 626 predefined methods:

```text
300 graph variants:
  5 distance topologies
  x 5 graph k values
  x 6 Laplacian regularization values
  x {direct y, cross-fitted-PLS-residual correction}

324 local Ridge variants:
  2 X representations (C1/C4)
  x 3 neighborhood distance topologies
  x 6 neighborhood sizes
  x 3 Ridge alphas
  x 3 inverse-distance powers

2 fixed anchors:
  GeoKNN10
  PLS4
```

Only the 16 target-matched splits receive the exhaustive search. The runner then performs
leave-one-split-out hyperparameter selection over that grid, selects three finalists per
graph/local family plus fixed anchors, and evaluates only those finalists on the 18 non-primary
stress splits.

Residual graph correction is leakage-safe with respect to pseudo-target y: the global PLS
anchor is fitted on visible labels, and the residual signal supplied to the graph is generated
by cross-fitting within the visible pseudo-training labels.

The actual 59 receive finalist candidate predictions only after validation. They are not the
final 04F output.


